From ef169992ec2c5d4a2ce83c82f87d8d0e0296aaf1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kirn Gill II <segin2005@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 9 Jun 2026 21:11:07 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 22/98] tcp: report POLLIN (EOF readable) on FIN-received
 states, not just POLLHUP
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

tcp_poll() for a connection in CLOSE_WAIT/CLOSING/LAST_ACK/TIME_WAIT with
a drained receive buffer returned POLLHUP without POLLIN.  POSIX and
Linux report such a socket as readable — read() returns 0 (EOF) without
blocking — and real software depends on that: libtirpc's svc_vc read_vc
loops `while ((revents & POLLIN) == 0)` around poll() and treats only a
poll *timeout* as fatal, so a POLLHUP-without-POLLIN fd makes poll()
return >0 forever and the RPC server spins at 100% CPU the instant a
client disconnects.  TCP_CLOSED gains POLLIN too (EOF is still readable
after full close).

Live-debugged on a wedged CDE session via the qemu gdbstub: opening dtcm
from the Front Panel froze all X11 input — cursor moved and changed
shape (server-side sprite tracking) but no client got events.  Thread
walk showed ttsession and rpc.ttdbserver burning the CPU in poll
(user-stack returns symbolized to read_vc svc_vc.c:541 ->
fill_input_buf xdr_rec.c:669), dtcm blocked in waitpid on a ttsession it
auto-spawned (which could never finish starting), and dtwm wedged behind
ToolTalk — its synchronous passive button grab then froze all input
queues.  AF_UNIX poll already had the correct readable-on-EOF semantics;
this brings TCP in line.

Verified on a copy of the live image: the same CDE+dtcm bring-up that
wedged (ttsession+ttdbserver at ~60% CPU, triple ttsession, dtcm in
waitpid) now idles — 16/16 on-CPU samples in swapper, single ttsession,
dtcm and X up.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sys/net/tcp.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sys/net/tcp.c b/sys/net/tcp.c
index e653e839..a1dacaa6 100644
--- a/sys/net/tcp.c
+++ b/sys/net/tcp.c
@@ -798,10 +798,27 @@ int tcp_poll(tcp_pcb_t *p, short events, void **wait_chan) {
         return 0;
     }
     if (p->state == TCP_CLOSED) {
-        return (events & POLLOUT ? POLLOUT : 0) | POLLHUP | POLLERR;
+        return (events & POLLOUT ? POLLOUT : 0) |
+               (events & POLLIN ? POLLIN : 0) | POLLHUP | POLLERR;
     }
     if (events & POLLIN) {
-        if (p->rx_count > 0) revents |= POLLIN;
+        if (p->rx_count > 0) {
+            revents |= POLLIN;
+        } else if (p->state == TCP_CLOSE_WAIT || p->state == TCP_CLOSING ||
+                   p->state == TCP_LAST_ACK   || p->state == TCP_TIME_WAIT) {
+            /*
+             * Peer has sent FIN (receive side closed) and the receive
+             * buffer is drained: a read() returns 0 (EOF) rather than
+             * blocking, so the socket IS readable.  POSIX and Linux report
+             * POLLIN here (not just POLLHUP).  libtirpc's svc_vc read_vc
+             * loops `while ((revents & POLLIN) == 0)` and only treats a
+             * poll *timeout* as fatal, so a POLLHUP-without-POLLIN fd makes
+             * poll() return >0 forever and the RPC server spins at 100%
+             * the moment a client disconnects — wedging ttsession /
+             * rpc.ttdbserver and hanging the whole CDE/ToolTalk startup.
+             */
+            revents |= POLLIN;
+        }
     }
     if (events & POLLOUT) {
         /* No tx buffering yet; treat as always-ready.  */
